issues.problematic_code_snippet:
class WebServer: WebServerProtocol, @unchecked Sendable {
    static let sharedInstance = WebServer()

    private let logger: Logger

    let server = GCDWebServer()

func handleServerTrust(
    challenge: URLAuthenticationChallenge,
    dispatchQueue: DispatchQueueInterface,
    completionHandler: @escaping @MainActor (URLSession.AuthChallengeDisposition, URLCredential?) -> Void
) {
    dispatchQueue.async {
        let origin = "\(challenge.protectionSpace.host):\(challenge.protectionSpace.port)"

        guard let trust = challenge.protectionSpace.serverTrust,
              let cert = SecTrustCopyCertificateChain(trust) as? [SecCertificate],
              self.profile.certStore.containsCertificate(cert[0], forOrigin: origin)
        else {
            completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)
            return
        }

public func ensureMainThread<T>(execute work: @escaping @MainActor () -> T) -> T? {
    if Thread.isMainThread {
        return MainActor.assumeIsolated {
            return work()
        }
    } else {
        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            _ = work()
        }
        return nil
    }
}

func throttle(completion: @escaping @MainActor () -> Void) {
    lastExecutionTime = Date()
    guard threshold <= 0 || lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow < -threshold else { return }
    DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: completion)
}

findings.body:
The PR moves `lastExecutionTime = Date()` above the `guard`. The guard then checks `lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow < -threshold`, but that value was just set to now, so it is about 0 and never less than `-0.35`. For any positive threshold, `throttle` returns early every time and `completion` never runs. The default delay is 0.35s, so this affects all throttled work in `HomepageViewController`, `StoriesFeedViewController`, and `WindowSimpleTabsCoordinator`.

The existing `MainThreadThrottlerTests.testSecondCallAfterDelayThresholdCallsBothClosures` and `testMultipleFastConsecutiveCallsAreThrottledAndExecutedAtMostOneTime` would fail, since the first expectation is never fulfilled. The PR doesn't touch those tests, so it looks like they were not run.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Utils/MainThreadThrottler.swift" lines="25-28"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The intended debounce needs the previous timestamp to be compared before it is overwritten. Restore the original order: guard first, then assign `lastExecutionTime`.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Utils/MainThreadThrottler.swift" lines="25-28"}
func throttle(completion: @escaping @MainActor () -> Void) {
    guard threshold <= 0 || lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow < -threshold else { return }
    lastExecutionTime = Date()
    DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: completion)
}

`handleServerTrust` runs its body inside `dispatchQueue.async`, which is a background queue. The PR removes the `ensureMainThread { completionHandler(...) }` wrapper on the early-return branch and calls `completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)` directly. `completionHandler` is `@MainActor`, so this breaks the actor contract and can cause UI-state races or crashes in the WKNavigationDelegate path. The accept branch below still hops to main, so the two branches are now inconsistent.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/Extensions/BrowserViewController+WebViewDelegates.swift" lines="1340-1346"}

The fix is to restore the main-thread hop on this branch, matching the accept branch and the pattern used elsewhere in this file.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/Extensions/BrowserViewController+WebViewDelegates.swift" lines="1340-1346"}
    else {
        ensureMainThread {
            completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)
        }
        return
    }

The PR changes `final class WebServer` to `class WebServer`. This is not part of the `@Sendable` cleanup and the description does not mention it. Removing `final` widens the type's API surface and allows subclassing. I didn't find a subclass in the repo, so nothing breaks today, but the change is unexplained and would normally be a separate decision.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Application/WebServer.swift" lines="15-20"}

Restore `final` unless the subclassing is intentional, in which case the PR should say so.

The new typealias has two spaces between `@MainActor` and the parenthesis. The rest of the codebase uses a single space, and this is a small whitespace nit that a linter or formatter would flag.

